WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:07.220
A coastal dune plant stops at a northern limit.

2
00:00:08.300 --> 00:00:11.680
Plants were established about 60 km beyond that limit.

3
00:00:12.620 --> 00:00:16.820
Twelve to fourteen years later, that population had more than tripled.

4
00:00:17.380 --> 00:00:19.220
So the plant can live there.

5
00:00:19.900 --> 00:00:21.380
Why had it never got there?

6
00:00:22.183 --> 00:00:24.483
The answer starts with the niche.

7
00:00:25.403 --> 00:00:28.023
Hutchinson gave it this form in 1957.

8
00:00:29.323 --> 00:00:35.463
A niche is the set of conditions and resources in which a population can persist without immigrants.

10
00:00:36.903 --> 00:00:39.923
Temperature and soil moisture are two axes of that space.

11
00:00:41.423 --> 00:00:43.603
Niche space is the space of conditions.

12
00:00:44.663 --> 00:00:46.163
Geographic space is the map.

13
00:00:47.203 --> 00:00:51.243
A species can be absent from a suitable place because it has not reached it.

14
00:00:51.933 --> 00:00:53.633
How do we test persistence?

15
00:00:54.673 --> 00:00:57.513
We use lambda, the finite rate of increase.

16
00:00:58.613 --> 00:01:02.613
Lambda is the factor by which a population multiplies in one year.

17
00:01:03.213 --> 00:01:07.233
If lambda is one or more, the population replaces itself.

18
00:01:08.253 --> 00:01:09.913
A sink population is different.

19
00:01:10.513 --> 00:01:14.033
There, deaths exceed births, and immigrants keep it going.

20
00:01:14.733 --> 00:01:19.293
So presence on a map, by itself, does not show where the niche ends.

21
00:01:20.217 --> 00:01:22.937
A range edge could have three causes.

22
00:01:23.917 --> 00:01:25.677
Conditions beyond it could be wrong.

23
00:01:26.377 --> 00:01:28.037
That is an abiotic limit.

24
00:01:29.017 --> 00:01:30.537
Other species could be in the way.

25
00:01:31.117 --> 00:01:32.457
That is a biotic limit.

26
00:01:33.217 --> 00:01:35.857
Or the species may simply not have arrived.

27
00:01:36.297 --> 00:01:37.777
That is a dispersal limit.

28
00:01:38.737 --> 00:01:43.117
The direct test is to plant the species beyond the edge, and follow it.

29
00:01:43.850 --> 00:01:47.950
A good test plants the same way inside the range.

30
00:01:48.550 --> 00:01:53.730
If the plantings inside fail too, the cause is handling or a bad year.

31
00:01:54.770 --> 00:01:57.630
Then cross the planting with neighbour removal.

32
00:01:58.330 --> 00:02:01.830
Clear half the plots, chosen at random, and leave half.

33
00:02:02.970 --> 00:02:05.230
Last, measure the whole life cycle.

34
00:02:05.970 --> 00:02:11.050
A fall in first-year growth does not show that a population cannot replace itself.

35
00:02:11.050 --> 00:02:13.030
Estimate lambda.

36
00:02:13.683 --> 00:02:15.323
Now read the result.

37
00:02:16.363 --> 00:02:23.083
If lambda is at least one beyond the range, with or without neighbours, the species can persist there.

39
00:02:23.843 --> 00:02:25.443
Dispersal is the likely limit.

40
00:02:26.383 --> 00:02:29.683
That is the inference drawn for the dune plant, at one site.

41
00:02:30.763 --> 00:02:35.903
If lambda falls below one only where neighbours remain, a biotic limit is

42
00:02:35.903 --> 00:02:41.723
implicated. Suppose lambda falls below one in every treatment beyond the range.

43
00:02:41.723 --> 00:02:47.943
If the inside controls stay at one or above, an abiotic limit is the likeliest.

44
00:02:48.903 --> 00:02:54.703
Keep this rule. Absence on a map, by itself, does not show where the niche ends.
